.TH "JNEXT" "1" "" "jnext" "jnext Manual"
.SH NAME
jnext \- real\-time ZX Spectrum Next emulator with an integrated
debugger
.SH SYNOPSIS
\f[B]jnext\f[R] [\f[I]options\f[R]] [\f[I]file\f[R]]
.SH DESCRIPTION
\f[B]jnext\f[R] is a real\-time, cross\-platform ZX Spectrum Next
emulator written in C++17 and derived from the official VHDL sources of
the ZX Spectrum Next FPGA core.
It emulates the Next itself as well as the ZX Spectrum 48K, 128K and
+2A/+3 machines, and carries a debugger that shows CPU, memory, video
layers, sprites, audio and NextREG state live.
.PP
\f[I]file\f[R] is a program to load, exactly equivalent to
\f[B]\-\-load\f[R] \f[I]FILE\f[R], so \f[CR]jnext game.tap\f[R] just
works.
Anything starting with \f[CR]\-\f[R] is treated as an option, never as a
filename.
Name the program one way or the other, not both at once:
\f[CR]jnext \-\-load game.tap game.tap\f[R] is rejected, and so is
naming two files.
.PP
The examples below use the Qt6 GUI build
(\f[CR]build/gui\-release/jnext\f[R]).
The SDL\-only build (\f[CR]build/sdl\-release/jnext\f[R]) takes the same
options minus the GUI and debugger ones.
.SH OPTIONS
.SS Machine and program
.TP
\f[B]\-\-machine\f[R] \f[I]TYPE\f[R]
Machine type: \f[CR]48k\f[R], \f[CR]128k\f[R], \f[CR]plus3\f[R],
\f[CR]next\f[R] (default).
.TP
\f[B]\-\-load\f[R] \f[I]FILE\f[R]
Load a program.
The format is detected from the extension: \f[CR].nex\f[R],
\f[CR].sna\f[R], \f[CR].szx\f[R], \f[CR].z80\f[R], \f[CR].tap\f[R],
\f[CR].tzx\f[R], \f[CR].wav\f[R], \f[CR].rzx\f[R].
(\f[CR].rzx\f[R] is accepted here and plays back, as
\f[B]\-\-rzx\-play\f[R].)
.TP
\f[B]\-\-nex\-args\f[R] \f[I]LINE\f[R]
Argument line for a NEX \f[B]V1.3\f[R] program.
\f[I]LINE\f[R] is placed, verbatim and zero\-terminated, in the CLI
buffer the file\(cqs header declares (its address and size), and
\f[CR]DE\f[R] points at it when the program starts.
A line as long as or longer than that buffer is truncated to the
buffer\(cqs size with no terminator \- which is what the reference V1.3
loader does.
Quote \f[I]LINE\f[R] to pass more than one word:
\f[CR]jnext \-\-load game.nex \-\-nex\-args \(dqlevel 3\(dq\f[R].
Only V1.3 declares a CLI buffer, so the option is inert \- with a
warning \- for a \f[CR]V1.0\f[R]\-\f[CR]V1.2\f[R] file, or a V1.3 file
whose header declares no buffer.
.TP
\f[B]\-\-sdcard\f[R] \f[I]FILE\f[R]
Mount SD\-card image \f[I]FILE\f[R] (\f[CR].img\f[R]).
Optional; see \f[B]SD CARD AND ROMS\f[R].
.TP
\f[B]\-\-sdcard\-download\-confirm\f[R]
Skip the download prompt for the default\-location image and proceed.
.TP
\f[B]\-\-sdcard\-download\-force\f[R]
Force re\-download and re\-patch of the default\-location image, to
recover a corrupted one.
Ignored when \f[B]\-\-sdcard\f[R] is given: an explicit path always
wins.
.TP
\f[B]\-\-sdcard\-readonly\f[R]
Open the SD image read\-only, so the host file is never modified.
The emulated machine sees a write\-protected card: writes are rejected
with the SD write\-error token rather than silently discarded.
Use it when a run must not disturb an image other runs share.
.TP
\f[B]\-\-speed\f[R] \f[I]PERCENT\f[R]
Emulator throttle: 50 = half, 100 = normal, 200 = 2x, 400 = 4x.
Clamped to 10..1000.
.TP
\f[B]\-\-joy1\-source\f[R] \f[I]SRC\f[R]
Host source for Joy 1 (port 0x1F): \f[CR]sdl\f[R] (autodetected gamepad,
default) or \f[CR]keys\f[R] (host arrow keys, Space = fire).
.TP
\f[B]\-\-joy2\-source\f[R] \f[I]SRC\f[R]
Host source for Joy 2 (port 0x37): \f[CR]sdl\f[R] (default) or
\f[CR]keys\f[R].
Only one connector may use \f[CR]keys\f[R].
Interactive (SDL/Qt) frontends only; inert under \f[B]\-\-headless\f[R].
.TP
\f[B]\-\-tape\-realtime\f[R]
Real\-time tape loading, at the speed of an actual tape, instead of fast
load.
.TP
\f[B]\-\-tape\-save\f[R] \f[I]FILE\f[R]
Append blocks SAVEd through the 48K ROM SA\-BYTES routine to
\f[I]FILE\f[R] (\f[CR].tap\f[R]).
Trap\-based: it fires when the ROM save routine at 0x04C2 runs with ROM
paged at slot 0.
Without this option no SAVE capture happens.
.TP
\f[B]\-\-esxdos\-stub\f[R]
Intercept \f[CR]RST $08\f[R] calls and provide in\-memory config I/O
plus \f[CR].RUN\f[R] sibling\-NEX chaining, without booting NextZXOS.
.TP
\f[B]\-\-rtc\f[R] \f[I]\(lqYYYY\-MM\-DD HH:MM:SS\(rq\f[R]
Pin the RTC to a fixed date and time (a frozen clock) instead of
following the host clock, which makes boot screenshots deterministic.
The ISO \f[CR]YYYY\-MM\-DDTHH:MM:SS\f[R] form is also accepted.
.TP
\f[B]\-\-silent\f[R]
Disable all sound output (beeper, the three AY/YM chips, DAC/Covox/
Specdrum).
No audio device is opened and the emulator skips PSG and mixer sample
synthesis entirely, which can measurably speed up CPU\-bound runs.
Tape loading (EAR input) is unaffected.
.TP
\f[B]\-\-inject\f[R] \f[I]FILE\f[R]
Load a raw binary into RAM.
.TP
\f[B]\-\-inject\-org\f[R] \f[I]ADDR\f[R]
Load address for \f[B]\-\-inject\f[R] (hex, default \f[CR]8000\f[R]).
.TP
\f[B]\-\-inject\-pc\f[R] \f[I]ADDR\f[R]
Entry point for \f[B]\-\-inject\f[R] (hex, default: same as
\f[B]\-\-inject\-org\f[R]).
.TP
\f[B]\-\-inject\-delay\f[R] \f[I]N\f[R]
Wait \f[I]N\f[R] frames before injecting (default 0).
Use around 100 if the binary calls ROM routines that need the system
variables set up first.
.SS Networking (ESP\-01 WiFi)
.TP
\f[B]\-\-esp\f[R]
Enable the emulated ESP\-01 WiFi module on UART 0.
\f[B]Off by default\f[R] \- see \f[B]NETWORKING\f[R] below for what
turning it on gives the running program.
.TP
\f[B]\-\-no\-esp\f[R]
Force the ESP off for this run, overriding a saved GUI preference that
enables it.
Given both, whichever comes last on the command line wins.
.TP
\f[B]\-\-esp\-allow\f[R] \f[I]HOST\f[R]
Only let the running program connect to \f[I]HOST\f[R].
Repeatable: give the option once per host.
\f[I]HOST\f[R] is a single name \- a comma is \f[B]rejected\f[R] rather
than taken as a separator or as part of the name, because a name that
silently matched nothing would look like a restriction that is in force
when it is not.
Matching is exact and case\-insensitive, with no wildcards, and an IP
address must be listed as itself.
With no \f[B]\-\-esp\-allow\f[R] at all the program may name any host
(subject to the always\-refused addresses in \f[B]NETWORKING\f[R]).
Requires \f[B]\-\-esp\f[R].
.SS Recording and playback
.TP
\f[B]\-\-record\f[R] \f[I]FILE\f[R]
Record video and audio to an MP4.
Requires \f[B]ffmpeg\f[R](1) on the PATH.
If the recording cannot be produced (encoder failure, no usable output),
\f[B]jnext\f[R] reports the error and exits non\-zero.
If the recording cannot even be started (no usable \f[B]ffmpeg\f[R]),
\f[B]jnext\f[R] exits non\-zero immediately, without running the
emulation.
.TP
\f[B]\-\-wav\-record\f[R] \f[I]FILE\f[R]
Record the mixed stereo output to a 44.1 kHz, 16\-bit PCM WAV.
Works headless and requires neither an audio device nor ffmpeg.
.TP
\f[B]\-\-dac\-trace\f[R] \f[I]FILE\f[R]
Record \f[CR]segment,tstate,channel,value\f[R] rows for physical DAC
writes.
A cold boot starts a new segment.
.TP
\f[B]\-\-audio\-gain\-db\f[R] \f[I]DB\f[R]
Set master host audio gain from \-24 dB to +24 dB (default 0 dB).
The gain is applied after the emulated hardware mix; overflow saturates
at the 16\-bit PCM limits.
