.TP
\f[B]\-\-delayed\-screenshot\-layers\f[R] \f[I]LIST\f[R]
Layers to compose into the screenshot: a comma\-separated list of
\f[CR]ula\f[R], \f[CR]layer2\f[R], \f[CR]sprites\f[R], \f[CR]tiles\f[R],
\f[CR]all\f[R] (default \f[CR]all\f[R]).
.TP
\f[B]\-\-delayed\-automatic\-exit\f[R] \f[I]N\f[R]
Exit the emulator after \f[I]N\f[R] seconds.
.TP
\f[B]\-\-delayed\-automatic\-exit\-frames\f[R] \f[I]N\f[R]
Exit after \f[I]N\f[R] frames.
Overrides \f[B]\-\-delayed\-automatic\-exit\f[R].
.TP
\f[B]\-\-delayed\-snapshot\f[R] \f[I]FILE\f[R]
Headless only.
Save a snapshot after a delay in frames.
The format is chosen by the extension of \f[I]FILE\f[R]:
\f[CR].szx\f[R], \f[CR].nex\f[R], anything else \f[CR].sna\f[R].
.TP
\f[B]\-\-delayed\-snapshot\-frames\f[R] \f[I]N\f[R]
Delay in frames for \f[B]\-\-delayed\-snapshot\f[R] (default 0).
.TP
\f[B]\-\-delayed\-keypress\f[R] \f[I]SECS\f[R] \f[I]KEY\f[R]
Press \f[I]KEY\f[R] after \f[I]SECS\f[R] seconds.
Headless only, repeatable.
.TP
\f[B]\-\-delayed\-keypress\-frames\f[R] \f[I]N\f[R] \f[I]KEY\f[R]
Press \f[I]KEY\f[R] after \f[I]N\f[R] emulated frames.
This is the frames\-unit spelling of \f[B]\-\-delayed\-keypress\f[R],
not an override of it: both forms queue into the same list, so giving
both schedules two keypresses.
.TP
\f[B]\-\-delayed\-nmi\f[R] \f[I]SECS\f[R] \f[I]BUTTON\f[R]
Press an NMI \f[I]BUTTON\f[R] after \f[I]SECS\f[R] seconds.
Headless only, repeatable.
\f[I]BUTTON\f[R] is case\-insensitive and names which button to press,
spelled as the label on a real Next\(cqs case.
Of its three buttons, two raise an NMI: \f[CR]nmi\f[R] (aliases
\f[CR]mf\f[R], \f[CR]m1\f[R]) is the \f[B]NMI\f[R] button, wired to the
Multiface; \f[CR]drive\f[R] (alias \f[CR]divmmc\f[R]) is the
\f[B]DRIVE\f[R] button, wired to the DivMMC.
\f[B]RESET\f[R] is not an NMI button and is not accepted here.
The press goes through the same path as the host F9 / F10 hotkeys, so it
is subject to the same enable gates \(em NextREG 0x06 bit 3 for the
Multiface, bit 4 plus NextREG 0x83 bit 0 for the DivMMC \(em and a press
with its gate closed does nothing, exactly as on hardware.
One press generates one NMI, not a repeating one.
.TP
\f[B]\-\-delayed\-nmi\-frames\f[R] \f[I]N\f[R] \f[I]BUTTON\f[R]
Press \f[I]BUTTON\f[R] after \f[I]N\f[R] emulated frames.
This is the frames\-unit spelling of \f[B]\-\-delayed\-nmi\f[R], not an
override of it: both forms queue into the same list, so giving both
schedules two presses.
.TP
\f[B]\-\-compositor\-trace\f[R] \f[I]FILE\f[R]
Dump a per\-pixel compositor trace (CSV) for one frame.
.TP
\f[B]\-\-compositor\-trace\-frame\f[R] \f[I]N\f[R]
Target frame for \f[B]\-\-compositor\-trace\f[R] (default 250).
.PP
\f[I]KEY\f[R] is case\-insensitive and is one of: a single character
(\f[CR]a\f[R]\-\f[CR]z\f[R], \f[CR]0\f[R]\-\f[CR]9\f[R], \f[CR].\f[R],
\f[CR],\f[R], \f[CR];\f[R], \f[CR]:\f[R]), one of the symbolic names
\f[CR]ENTER\f[R], \f[CR]RETURN\f[R], \f[CR]SPACE\f[R], \f[CR]UP\f[R],
\f[CR]DOWN\f[R], \f[CR]LEFT\f[R], \f[CR]RIGHT\f[R], or a compound
\f[CR]sym+\f[R]\f[I]char\f[R] / \f[CR]caps+\f[R]\f[I]char\f[R] (for
example \f[CR]sym+m\f[R] is \f[CR].\f[R]).
