.SH MACHINES
.TP
\f[CR]48k\f[R]
ZX Spectrum 48K \- the original rubber\-key Spectrum.
.TP
\f[CR]128k\f[R]
ZX Spectrum 128K \- AY sound and memory paging.
.TP
\f[CR]plus3\f[R]
ZX Spectrum +2A/+3 \- extended paging.
.TP
\f[CR]next\f[R]
ZX Spectrum Next (Issue 2) \- full Next hardware and extended features.
.PP
The machine type can also be switched at runtime from the GUI
(\f[B]Machine > Machine Type\f[R]); switching resets the machine.
.SH SD CARD AND ROMS
jnext takes its ROMs from an SD\-card image, exactly like real ZX
Spectrum Next hardware.
There are two parts to it:
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]The FPGA boot ROM\f[R] (\f[CR]nextboot.rom\f[R], 8 KB) is
\f[I]silicon\-baked\f[R]: embedded in the jnext binary at link time.
No flag, no lookup; it mirrors the on\-FPGA flash IPL of the real
machine.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]Everything else\f[R] \- 48K / 128K / +3 BASIC, NextZXOS, DivMMC
firmware, Multiface firmware \- is read from the SD\-card image at the
canonical TBBlue paths under \f[CR]/MACHINES/NEXT/\f[R]
(\f[CR]48.rom\f[R], \f[CR]128.rom\f[R], \f[CR]plus3.rom\f[R],
\f[CR]enNxtmmc.rom\f[R], \f[CR]enNextMf.rom\f[R]).
.PP
So even a \f[B]\-\-machine\f[R] \f[CR]48k\f[R] run needs an SD\-card
image: that is where \f[CR]48.rom\f[R] lives.
.PP
Point at an image with \f[B]\-\-sdcard\f[R] \f[I]FILE\f[R], or give no
\f[B]\-\-sdcard\f[R] at all: jnext then falls back to
\f[CR]\(ti/.jnext/sdcard/cspect\-next\-1gb\-fixed.img\f[R] (a
FAT32\-patched image), offering to download the canonical distribution
image (kept alongside it as \f[CR]cspect\-next\-1gb.img\f[R]) and
produce that patched copy on first run.
\f[B]\-\-sdcard\-download\-confirm\f[R] skips the prompt, which is
useful in scripts; \f[B]\-\-sdcard\-download\-force\f[R] re\-downloads
and re\-patches a corrupted cached image.
An explicit \f[B]\-\-sdcard\f[R] always wins over both.
.PP
Note that jnext opens the image \f[B]read\-write\f[R] by default, and
writes the emulated machine makes to the SD card are persisted to the
file.
Two runs sharing one image are therefore not independent: a run that
boots NextZXOS mutates the image the next run reads.
For a reproducible run, either pass \f[B]\-\-sdcard\-readonly\f[R], or
give it a private copy (\f[CR]cp \-\-reflink=auto\f[R] is instant and
costs no space on a copy\-on\-write filesystem).
.PP
The mounted image is also the SD card the emulated machine sees at
runtime, through the SPI/DivMMC path: NextZXOS reads its files from it.
.SH LOADING PROGRAMS
Use \f[B]\-\-load\f[R] \f[I]FILE\f[R], or simply a bare filename
(\f[CR]jnext game.tap\f[R]).
The two are equivalent, so use one or the other: combining them
(\f[CR]jnext \-\-load game.tap game.tap\f[R]) is rejected, as is naming
two files.
The format is detected from the extension:
.TP
\f[CR].nex\f[R]
v1.0/1.1/1.2; pages, Layer 2 screen and palette from the header.
.TP
\f[CR].sna\f[R]
48K and 128K snapshots.
.TP
\f[CR].szx\f[R]
Chunked snapshots, zlib\-compressed pages.
.TP
\f[CR].z80\f[R]
v1/v2/v3, 48K and 128K, RLE\-compressed or raw pages.
.TP
\f[CR].tap\f[R]
Fast load via a ROM trap; \f[CR]LOAD \(dq\(dq\f[R] is auto\-typed.
.TP
\f[CR].tzx\f[R]
Full block support; fast load or real\-time.
.TP
\f[CR].wav\f[R]
RIFF/PCM EAR\-bit playback (8/16\-bit, mono/stereo).
.TP
\f[CR].rzx\f[R]
Played back as if \f[B]\-\-rzx\-play\f[R] had been given.
.PP
\f[CR].tap\f[R] and \f[CR].tzx\f[R] load in real time instead with
\f[B]\-\-tape\-realtime\f[R].
.PP
RZX recording uses \f[B]\-\-rzx\-record\f[R] \f[I]FILE\f[R].
The GUI\(cqs \f[B]File > Load NEX File\&...\f[R] dialog accepts all of
the above.
.PP
\f[B]\-\-esxdos\-stub\f[R] handles the common \f[CR]RST $08\f[R] calls
used by directly loaded NEX programs.
It provides one in\-memory file and \f[CR]run sibling.nex\f[R] chaining.
.PP
Raw binaries go straight into RAM with \f[B]\-\-inject\f[R]
\f[I]FILE\f[R] (\f[B]\-\-inject\-org\f[R] load address,
\f[B]\-\-inject\-pc\f[R] entry point, \f[B]\-\-inject\-delay\f[R] a
frame delay before injecting).
.SH HEADLESS MODE, SCREENSHOTS AND RECORDING
\f[B]\-\-headless\f[R] runs the emulator with no window and no audio
device, as fast as the host allows: the mode built for scripting and CI.
.PP
Notes worth knowing:
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]\-\-delayed\-screenshot\-frames\f[R] is the deterministic one.
Frame counts are reproducible; wall\-clock seconds are not.
\f[B]\-\-delayed\-automatic\-exit\-frames\f[R] is the same idea for the
exit bound, and wins over \f[B]\-\-delayed\-automatic\-exit\f[R] when
both are given.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]\-\-rtc\f[R] makes boot screenshots reproducible by freezing the
clock, so the NextZXOS date and time on screen never change between
runs.
.IP \(bu 2
A screenshot that was asked for and never taken is an error.
If \f[B]\-\-delayed\-automatic\-exit\f[R] fires before the capture comes
due, jnext logs an error and exits non\-zero instead of silently writing
nothing.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]\-\-delayed\-screenshot\-layers\f[R] isolates a layer.
An excluded layer is composed as if its hardware enable bit were clear,
so the remaining ones still follow the NR 0x15 priority order and the NR
0x4A fallback colour shows through where everything is transparent.
Excluding \f[CR]ula\f[R] also removes the border: the ULA is what draws
it.
.IP \(bu 2
Video recording (\f[B]\-\-record\f[R] \f[I]FILE\f[R], or \f[B]File >
Record MPEG4 Video\f[R]) pipes video and audio to ffmpeg, which must be
installed.
.IP \(bu 2
WAV recording (\f[B]\-\-wav\-record\f[R] \f[I]FILE\f[R]) captures the
mixed audio before the host playback device.
It works in headless mode and continues through cold boots such as
sibling\-NEX chaining.
It cannot be combined with \f[B]\-\-silent\f[R], which disables mixer
synthesis.
If the emulator exits abnormally, the file may retain its initial
zero\-length data header even though PCM follows it.
.IP \(bu 2
DAC tracing (\f[B]\-\-dac\-trace\f[R] \f[I]FILE\f[R]) records guest DAC
writes before mixing.
Channels are numbered 0\-3 (A\-D) and timestamps are CPU T\-states.
.IP \(bu 2
Master and per\-subsystem host gains are available under \f[B]Settings >
Preferences > Audio\f[R], or for one run with the
\f[B]\-\-audio\-gain\-\f[R] family.
Beeper, each TurboSound AY chip and the DAC family can be balanced
independently before the master gain.
They change playback and recording volume without changing
guest\-visible audio state and survive cold boots.
Each explicit CLI value overrides its corresponding saved preference.
.SH NETWORKING (ESP\-01 WiFi)
A real ZX Spectrum Next has a header for an ESP\-01 WiFi module on UART
0.
\f[B]\-\-esp\f[R] emulates one, so software written for it \- NXtel,
nextsync, \f[CR]newt\f[R], the NextZXOS \f[CR].HTTP\f[R] and
\f[CR].zxdb\-dl\f[R] dot commands \- can reach the network through your
host.
.PP
Both \f[B]TCP and UDP\f[R] are emulated.
UDP is what \f[CR]newt\f[R] uses to read the time from an internet SNTP
server, so \f[CR].newt sntp 0 pool.ntp.org\f[R] works; the optional
local\-port argument of \f[CR]AT+CIPSTART=\(dqUDP\(dq,...\f[R] is
honoured.
UDP \(lqmodes\(rq 1 and 2, which let the far end of a link become
whoever last sent a packet, are answered \f[CR]ERROR\f[R] rather than
accepted and ignored.
TLS, server/listen mode, multiplexed connections and transparent mode
are not emulated.
.SS It is off by default, and that is the point
An enabled ESP gives the running program an \f[B]outbound TCP and UDP
pipe out of the emulator\f[R], and the program can already read the
SD\-card image, which jnext opens read\-write.
A program you did not write is a program you are trusting when you turn
this on.
So jnext does not turn it on for you, and there is no way for a loaded
program to turn it on for itself.
.PP
When it \f[I]is\f[R] on, a windowed run shows an \f[B]ESP cell in the
status bar\f[R] for as long as the run lasts.
Its presence means the guest can reach the network; it names the host of
the most recent connection, and shows an allowlist refusal or a fault in
red.
There is no way to have the ESP enabled in a window without that cell
being there.
\f[B]\-\-headless\f[R] builds no window and so has no status bar: there
the \f[CR]esp01\f[R] log is the only report, which is why it reports
every connection at the default level.
.PP
An address the policy below refuses is reported as a \f[I]failure\f[R]
rather than an allowlist refusal, so it is not red; the reason
(\f[CR]address refused by policy: \&...\f[R]) is in the cell\(cqs
tooltip and in the log.
See \c
.UR https://github.com/jorgegv/jnext/issues/161
issue #161
.UE \c
\&.
.SS What is always refused, whatever you allow
These are refused by address, before any connection is attempted, and
\f[B]\-\-esp\-allow\f[R] cannot re\-enable them:
.IP \(bu 2
loopback \- \f[CR]127.0.0.0/8\f[R] and \f[CR]::1\f[R], i.e.\ daemons on
your own machine;
.IP \(bu 2
link\-local \- \f[CR]169.254.0.0/16\f[R] and \f[CR]fe80::/10\f[R];
.IP \(bu 2
cloud metadata endpoints \- \f[CR]169.254.169.254\f[R],
\f[CR]100.100.100.200\f[R] and \f[CR]fd00:ec2::254\f[R];
.IP \(bu 2
the unspecified addresses \f[CR]0.0.0.0/8\f[R] and \f[CR]::\f[R];
.IP \(bu 2
multicast and reserved space \- \f[CR]224.0.0.0/4\f[R],
\f[CR]240.0.0.0/4\f[R], \f[CR]255.255.255.255\f[R], \f[CR]ff00::/8\f[R].
.PP
IPv6 spellings of an IPv4 address (\f[CR]::ffff:127.0.0.1\f[R], NAT64,
6to4) are resolved to what they actually reach and judged on that, so
they cannot be used to get around the list.
.PP
\f[B]Your own LAN is reachable.\f[R] RFC1918 addresses
(\f[CR]192.168.x.x\f[R], \f[CR]10.x.x.x\f[R],
\f[CR]172.16\-31.x.x\f[R]), carrier\-grade NAT
(\f[CR]100.64.0.0/10\f[R]) and IPv6 unique\-local (\f[CR]fc00::/7\f[R])
are deliberately \f[I]not\f[R] refused \- reaching a machine on your own
network is the main thing people use this for, and nextsync in
particular connects to a PC on the LAN.
The two cloud\-metadata addresses above sit inside those ranges and are
carved back out.
.SS Narrowing it further
\f[B]\-\-esp\-allow\f[R] restricts the program to hosts you name:
.IP
.EX
jnext \-\-esp \-\-esp\-allow nx.nxtel.org NXtel.nex
.EE
.PP
Every connection to anything else is refused, the program gets the same
\f[CR]ERROR\f[R] it would get from a real module that could not connect,
and the refusal is reported in the log and in the status bar.
.SS Making it permanent
\f[B]Settings > Preferences > Network\f[R] holds the same two settings
for people who run one of these programs regularly: an enable checkbox
and the allowed\-host list, one host per line (a comma separates too,
matching the config file\(cqs own format).
Blank entries and repeats are dropped, and the list is greyed out while
the module is off, because it restricts nothing on its own.
.PP
The module is built when a machine starts, so this page cannot switch it
on or off underneath a running program: the change takes effect on the
next hard reset (\f[B]F1\f[R], or \f[B]Machine > Power Reset\f[R]) and
on the next launch.
The CLI still wins for a single run in both directions \-
\f[B]\-\-esp\f[R] over a saved \f[I]off\f[R], and \f[B]\-\-no\-esp\f[R]
over a saved \f[I]on\f[R].
.SS There is no server mode yet
The emulated module cannot listen for incoming connections \-
\f[CR]AT+CIPSERVER\f[R] is not implemented \f[B]yet\f[R], so today there
is no inbound attack surface at all.
.PP
That is a scoping decision, not a permanent one.
The command set jnext emulates is the one \f[I]evidenced\f[R] in
software that actually runs on a Next, and \f[CR]AT+CIPSERVER\f[R]
appears in all of it exactly once, only to turn itself off.
Emulating the AT firmware down to the datasheet \- server mode, UDP,
multiplexed connections and transparent mode included \- is tracked as
its own piece of work (issue #154).
Expect the security notes above to grow an inbound half when it lands.
.SS Nothing about your host leaks into the program
The module reports a fixed synthetic identity: SSID
\f[CR]JNextWifiHost\f[R], a \f[CR]02:00:00:...\f[R] MAC and BSSID, and a
\f[CR]192.168.1.50\f[R] station address.
None of it is read from your machine, so a program cannot learn your
real SSID, MAC or local addresses by asking.
The emulated module is not a radio and never scans.
.SH LOGGING
\f[B]\-\-log\-level\f[R] \f[I]SPEC\f[R] sets per\-subsystem log levels.
A bare level sets every subsystem; a
\f[I]name\f[R]\f[CR]=\f[R]\f[I]level\f[R] pair sets one.
They can be mixed, and are applied left to right:
.IP
.EX
jnext \-\-log\-level warn                      # everything at warn
jnext \-\-log\-level cpu=trace                 # just the CPU
jnext \-\-log\-level warn,emulator=debug       # quiet, except the emulator
.EE
.PP
Levels are \f[CR]trace\f[R], \f[CR]debug\f[R], \f[CR]info\f[R]
(default), \f[CR]warn\f[R], \f[CR]err\f[R], \f[CR]critical\f[R] and
\f[CR]off\f[R]; \f[CR]warning\f[R], \f[CR]error\f[R], \f[CR]fatal\f[R]
and \f[CR]none\f[R] are accepted as aliases.
.PP
Subsystems are \f[CR]cpu\f[R], \f[CR]memory\f[R], \f[CR]ula\f[R],
\f[CR]video\f[R], \f[CR]audio\f[R], \f[CR]port\f[R], \f[CR]nextreg\f[R],
\f[CR]dma\f[R], \f[CR]copper\f[R], \f[CR]uart\f[R], \f[CR]input\f[R],
\f[CR]platform\f[R], \f[CR]emulator\f[R], \f[CR]sdcard\f[R],
\f[CR]divmmc\f[R], \f[CR]spi\f[R], \f[CR]ctc\f[R], \f[CR]i2c\f[R],
\f[CR]multiface\f[R], \f[CR]esp01\f[R] and \f[CR]esxdos\f[R].
.PP
\f[CR]esp01\f[R] is the emulated ESP\-01 WiFi module
(\f[B]\-\-esp\f[R]).
At the default \f[CR]info\f[R] level it reports only the connections
opened, refused, failed and closed \- the events \f[B]NETWORKING\f[R]
describes as never silent.
A refusal or a failure is a warning, so it survives
\f[B]\-\-log\-level\f[R] \f[I]warn\f[R] too.
\f[CR]debug\f[R] adds every AT command received and every response
emitted, and \f[CR]trace\f[R] adds the raw byte traffic in both
directions.
Turning it down to \f[CR]off\f[R] silences the connection reports too,
which is a deliberate choice on the user\(cqs part and not something
jnext undoes.
.PP
\f[CR]esxdos\f[R] is a tracer rather than a subsystem: at
\f[CR]trace\f[R] level every \f[CR]RST $08\f[R] esxdos call is logged
with its arguments and its result, including calls jnext does not
service.
It does not need \f[B]\-\-esxdos\-stub\f[R] \(em the interesting case is
a NextZXOS\-expecting program running without the stub, where those
calls are otherwise invisible.
.SH PROFILING
\f[B]\-\-profile\f[R] enables the CPU T\-state profiler: one histogram
entry per executed instruction, written to \f[B]\-\-profile\-output\f[R]
(default \f[CR]profile.dat\f[R]) on exit.
Join it against a z88dk \f[CR].map\f[R] file to get a per\-function
heatmap:
.IP
.EX
jnext \-\-headless game.nex \-\-profile \-\-profile\-output game.dat \(rs
    \-\-delayed\-automatic\-exit 20
tools/get\-function\-heatmap.pl \-m game.map game.dat
.EE
.SH THE GUI
The Qt6 build gives a native window with menus, a toolbar and a status
bar (FPS, CPU speed, emulator speed, tape status, machine type), Hi\-DPI
pixel\-perfect rendering at integer scale, and a CRT scanline filter.
.PP
Menu shortcuts use \f[B]Alt\f[R], never plain \f[B]Ctrl\f[R]: Ctrl is
the Spectrum\(cqs Symbol Shift, so a Ctrl shortcut would eat a key the
guest needs (see \f[B]THE KEYBOARD\f[R] below).
.TP
\f[B]File\f[R]
Load a program (Alt+O \- NEX/SNA/SZX/TAP/TZX/WAV/RZX), Mount SD Card
Image, Record MPEG4 Video (Ctrl+F5) / Stop (Ctrl+F6), Play RZX / Record
RZX / Stop RZX, Save Screenshot (Alt+S), Save Snapshot (Alt+Shift+S),
Quit (Alt+Q).
.TP
\f[B]Machine\f[R]
Power Reset (Alt+R \- power off/on cold boot, full boot chain), Soft
Reset (F4 \- the front\-panel reset button: back to NextZXOS without
re\-running the boot chain; does nothing while the firmware still holds
config mode, or after a direct \f[B]\-\-load\f[R] where the firmware
never ran), Machine Type (48K / 128K / +3 / Next), CPU Speed (3.5 / 7 /
14 / 28 MHz \- the Next\(cqs own clock), Emulator Speed (0.5x / 1x / 2x
/ 4x / custom % \- the host\-side throttle).
.TP
\f[B]Input\f[R]
Joy 1 Source (port 0x1F) and Joy 2 Source (port 0x37), each selecting
what drives that connector; Capture Mouse, which confines the host
pointer so the Kempston mouse can move freely (Ctrl+Alt releases it).
.TP
\f[B]Tape\f[R]
Open Tape File (Alt+T), Eject, Rewind, Fast Load (toggle).
.TP
\f[B]Debug\f[R]
Magic Breakpoint (toggle).
.TP
\f[B]View\f[R]
Scale 1x / 2x / 3x, Fullscreen (F11, letterboxed), CRT Filter, Debugger
(Alt+D).
.TP
\f[B]Settings\f[R]
Preferences\&..., which opens the settings dialog (Startup, Input, Audio
and Paths tabs).
Its values are saved to the configuration file; command\-line options
always take precedence over them.
.TP
\f[B]Help\f[R]
About.
.PP
The toolbar has Power Reset, Soft Reset, Load, Screenshot, an NMI button
(Multiface NMI) and, in a debugger\-enabled build, a bug button that
opens the debugger.
.PP
CPU Speed and Emulator Speed are different things: the first changes the
clock the emulated Z80 runs at, which is a real Next feature; the second
changes how fast the emulator runs relative to real time.
